IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`
IMPORT `ccc16-dna-definitions`
IMPORT `ccc16-open-court`

-- ccc16-publication-bans.l4 -- sections 486.4 to 486.6 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (Part XV): orders restricting publication of the identity of a victim,
-- a witness or a justice system participant, the variation and revocation of those orders, and the offence of not complying with one.
-- Part agent ccc16. Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Imports the keel's core, ccc16-dna-definitions (the row type and the matcher of a list of offence provisions) and
-- ccc16-open-court (the factor macros' pattern is repeated there, not imported; `s 486.5(7)` here has its own).
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30).
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- Conventions: as in ccc16-open-court.l4 (a "may" with an "if" gate answers NOTHING on a failed gate; a "shall" answers JUST FALSE on a failed trigger or a met "unless";
-- a scope limit with no "only" answers NOTHING). "as soon as feasible" states no number, so none is invented: a duty with that wording is a function of whether it arises.
-- The terms of section 2 the text uses are asked as facts: `s 2 victim`, `s 2 justice system participant`, `s 2 terrorism offence`, `s 2 serious offence` and the criminal-organization limbs are MAYBE BOOLEAN inputs.

§ `Who applies: shared type`

DECLARE `s 486.4 who applies` IS ONE OF
    `s 486.4 the prosecutor`
    `s 486.4 the victim`
    `s 486.4 a witness under the age of 18 years`
    `s 486.4 a witness of the age of 18 years or more`
    `s 486.4 a justice system participant`
    `s 486.4 the accused`
    `s 486.4 another person`

§ `Section 486.4 -- Order restricting publication -- sexual offences`

-- 486.4(1)(a)(i): an offence under section 151, 152, ..., 346 or 347; (ii) any offence under this Act, as it read from time to time before the day on which this subparagraph comes into force, if the conduct alleged
-- would be an offence referred to in subparagraph (i) if it occurred on or after that day.
